{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966794,"nonce":"DTvEUSFZXzYBLdlZ7+SKBFrMXU0=","title":"CSS-inherited text colour vanished after a subtree rebuild — the subtree pass read parents","message":"A `color:` on an ancestor reaches text through a propagation pass, not\nthrough the painter: the pass fills `props.text_color` where it is\nunset, and paint prefers `props.text_color` over the text's own colour.\n\nThe subtree variant of that pass read each node's parent from the\n**element registry**. After a rebuild, a new node's registry parent can\nname something that is not its layout parent, and the lookup then found\nnothing to inherit and left the value unset. Text fell back to the\nlayout default, black, which on a dark scheme reads as the content\ndisappearing.\n\nThe tell: **the first build was correct and the rebuild was not**.\n`cn.Tabs` panels were themed until a tab was switched.\n\nFixed by carrying the inherited set down the **layout tree** from the\nsubtree root, seeded from what that root's parent resolved to, rather\nthan reading a parent per node. The full-tree pass still uses the flat\nform; it runs before any rebuild, where the two agree.\n\n**Diagnosing this kind of thing:** read `props.text_color` rather than\n`TextData.color` in a test. The pass never touches `TextData.color`, so\na probe on it shows black even when the text paints correctly. See\n[[gotcha-stateful-content-loses-css-inheritance]] for the same family of\nbug at the widget level.\n\n**A global `* { color: … }` is not the fix.** CSS colour overwrites\n`props.text_color` unconditionally, so a universal rule repaints every\nwidget's own labels too. Inheritance from a class rule only fills where\nnothing is set, so scope it to a container class.","files":null}]}